  "markdown_report": "The recommended experiment is a **terminal-loss curriculum within one BERT4Rec per category**. At 10% research progress, objective alignment offers a small, attributable change before architectural expansion.\n\n**Insights from the supplied evidence.** First, equal terminal/interior example counts need not give equal objective weights. Per-example reduction makes the intended balance explicit. Second, users reject broad associations and miss precise recent purposes or combined preferences. Training on complete prefixes could help the network learn these distinctions, although usefulness remains a hypothesis. Third, Electronics has a 24.46% sampled negative-beats-positive rate, while history sensitivity is unavailable everywhere. This motivates an objective experiment and parity repair, without establishing a serving defect or generalization failure.\n\n**Insights from related work.** First, [BERT4Rec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690) learns interior masked targets, whereas serving predicts an appended mask. [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) motivates investigating this mismatch but supplies no winning weight here. Second, [Sequential Masked Modeling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11150) uses augmentation and masking suited to sequential prediction. Its different evaluation setting makes transfer uncertain. Third, [Correcting the LogQ Correction](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09331) distinguishes deterministically inserted positives from sampled negatives. Consequently, objective alignment and negative-mining changes should be tested separately.\n\nThe conceptual framework has three directions: **supervision alignment** changes where targets occur; **candidate discrimination** changes competing training items; **context representation** changes how recent and repeated behavior interact. Serialization parity is a prerequisite across all three.\n\nThree atomic candidates follow. Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each assessment includes a benefit (+) and limitation (−).\n\n| Idea | Assessments |\n|---|---|\n| Terminal-weight curriculum | O 4: +controlled adaptation; −established principle. P 7: +reusable alignment study; −benchmark dependent. U 7: +recent purpose and combined preferences; −no compatibility guarantees. M 7: +tests weak discrimination; −margins may not improve. D 2: +loss-only change; −branch reduction requires care. |\n| Moderate hard-negative sampling | O 3: +targeted competition; −standard mining. P 6: +affordable; −false negatives. U 6: +distinguishes plausible alternatives; −scores do not establish usefulness. M 7: +targets poor margins; −changes diagnostic difficulty. D 4: +shared scorer; −sampling corrections complicate implementation. |\n| Relative-distance attention bias | O 3: +family-compatible context bias; −established technique. P 6: +tests temporal structure; −extra tuning. U 7: +recent/repeated context; −cannot infer satisfied needs. M 4: +testable sensitivity; −sensitivity currently unavailable. D 5: +one network; −predictor and probes require adaptation. |\n\nSelect the curriculum: retain equal weighting initially, then increase terminal weight linearly to 0.8. It targets two user-facing failures—broad matches outranking precise current purposes, and isolated attributes replacing preference conjunctions—while testing Electronics’ weak sampled discrimination. These are proposed mechanisms, not measured improvements. Compare against equal weighting using fixed macro validation HR@10; repair checkpoint parity before interpreting sensitivity.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Train one BERT4Rec per category with a gradual shift from equally weighted terminal-prefix and interior-mask losses toward terminal prediction. Keep the architecture, sampler endpoint distribution, negative sampling, optimizer, and serving path unchanged to isolate loss weighting.",
    "motivation": "Terminal-prefix training matches appended-mask inference and requires predictions from the observed prefix without future context. The hypothesis addresses broad associations outranking precise recent purposes and isolated shared attributes displacing preference conjunctions. Electronics’ observed 24.46% in-sample negative-beats-positive rate motivates testing discrimination, but neither better margins nor user-facing benefits are established.",
    "implementation_notes": "Use alpha(e)=0.5+0.3*min(e/max(1,E/2),1), where E is the configured maximum epoch count capped at 200. Average supervised-position losses within each example, then average examples separately within each branch; optimize alpha*terminal_mean+(1-alpha)*interior_mean. Keep exactly 200 batches per epoch, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four supplied datasets and routes, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. For terminal examples, sample an eligible training endpoint uniformly, remove its target occurrence and all later events, and append MASK; earlier repeated occurrences remain legitimate history. Interior targets must all be replaced by MASK. Preserve the supplied loss and negative proposal initially. Use final nonpadding-position readout under left padding. Save exactly one selected checkpoint per category and preserve the embedded predictor’s architecture and mask construction. Repair parity through an explicit importable model factory/exported loader rather than class-source introspection; verify exact state tensors and short/full/truncated/batched scoring. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and ModelView compatibility. Leave history sensitivity unavailable until parity passes. No extra metadata, pretrained weights, independent score components, or hidden labels are required. Treat the supplied 0.5425 baseline score as historical evidence, not a result of this proposal.",
    "pseudocode": "E = min(configured_epochs, 200)\nfor category in supplied_four_categories:\n    model = initialize_corrected_BERT4Rec(seed=42)\n    for epoch in range(E):\n        alpha = 0.5 + 0.3 * min(epoch / max(1, E / 2), 1.0)\n        for step in range(200):\n            terminal = sample_uniform_training_endpoints()\n            terminal.input = left_pad(truncate(prefix_before_endpoint, maxlen-1) + [MASK])\n            interior = sample_baseline_interior_examples(all_targets_replaced_by_MASK=True)\n            Lt = mean_examples(mean_supervised_positions(baseline_loss(model, terminal)))\n            Li = mean_examples(mean_supervised_positions(baseline_loss(model, interior)))\n            optimize(alpha * Lt + (1-alpha) * Li)\n        apply_supplied_validation_checkpoint_selection()\n    save_one_checkpoint_and_matching_configuration(category)\nwrite_matching_predictor_with_existing_category_routes()\nverify_exported_checkpoint_state_and_appended_mask_score_parity()\nrun_compatible_diagnostics_without_interpreting_unavailable_sensitivity()\n# Host independently evaluates fixed four-category macro validation HR@10.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "An explicit curriculum isolates terminal alignment within the corrected, single-model BERT4Rec setup.",
      "negative": "Curriculum weighting and train–inference alignment are established principles; novelty is incremental."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A controlled result could guide later masking and supervision studies with little architectural cost.",
      "negative": "The proposed endpoint weight is unvalidated, and sampled-pair improvements may not transfer to macro validation HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "The experimental change only requires branch-wise loss reduction and a deterministic weight schedule.",
      "negative": "Existing serialization-parity diagnostics still require repair, and unequal supervised-position counts can silently distort weighting."
    }
  },
